<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-precalc-ineq" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Inequalities</title>

  <p>
    Inequalities appear throughout calculus, especially when describing domains,
    intervals of increase or decrease, and where functions are positive or negative.
    Unlike equations, inequalities often have many solutions.
    In this section, we focus on a single systematic approach,
    the test point method, which works reliably for many types of inequalities.
  </p>

  <p>
    The key idea behind the test point method is simple.
    We first determine where an expression could change sign,
    then test representative points from each interval.
    This approach emphasizes understanding over memorization
    and reduces common algebra mistakes.
  </p>

  <!-- ======================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ssec-ineq-overview">
    <title>The Test Point Method: Overview</title>

    <p>
      The test point method is used to solve inequalities of the form
      <m>f(x) &gt; 0</m>, <m>f(x) \ge 0</m>, <m>f(x) &lt; 0</m>, or <m>f(x) \le 0</m>.
      Instead of solving directly, we analyze where the function is positive or negative.
    </p>

    <algorithm xml:id="pr-test-point-method">
      <statement>
        <p>
          To solve an inequality using test points:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Rewrite the inequality so that one side is zero.</li>
          <li>Find all values of <m>x</m> where the expression equals zero or is undefined.</li>
          <li>Use these values to divide the number line into intervals.</li>
          <li>Choose one test point from each interval and determine the sign of the expression.</li>
          <li>State your conclusion: Determine which intervals satisfy the inequality.</li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>


  </subsection>

  <!-- ======================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="ssec-ineq-examples">
    <title>Several Examples</title>
    <p> Now let's see this modeled in several examples!</p>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-ineq-linear">
    <title>Linear Example</title>      
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>2x - 4 &gt; 0</m>. Write your answer in interval notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p> 
          <alert>Step 1:</alert> Rewrite the inequality so one side is zero (already done).
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Step 2:</alert> Identify where the expression equals zero or is undefined.
        <me>
          2x - 4 = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x = 2.
        </me>
        There are no values of <m>x</m> where the expression is undefined.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          <alert>Step 3:</alert> Use <m>x = 2</m> to divide the number line into intervals <m>(-\infty, 2)</m> and <m>(2, \infty)</m>. 
          Visually this looks like:
        </p>


          <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided at x equals 2.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
	\begin{tikzpicture}
		\draw[<->, thick] (-2,0)-- (2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- (6,0) node[right] {$x$};
	\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>




        <p>
          <alert>Step 4:</alert> Choose a test point from each interval and determine the sign of the expression.
        </p>
        <ul><li>
          For the interval <m>(-\infty, 2)</m>, we can test <m>x = 0</m>. Plugging this into 
          our expression gives <m>2(0) - 4 = -4</m>, which is negative.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(2, \infty)</m>, we can test <m>x = 3</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>2(3) - 4 = 2</m>, which is positive.
        </li></ul>

          <p>Visually, this looks like:</p>

                    <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided at x equals 2. The interval to the left of 2 is labeled negative, 
    while the interval to the right of 2 is labeled positive. Test points at x equals 0 and x equals 3 are marked with their 
    respective signs.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
	\begin{tikzpicture}
		\draw[<->, thick] (-2,0)-- (2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- (6,0) node[right] {$x$};
		\draw (0,0) 
		node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(-)$}
		node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$0$};
		\draw (4,0) 
		node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(+)$}
		node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$3$};
	\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>
        

        <p>
          <alert>Step 5:</alert> State your conclusion: Choose the interval(s) where the expression is positive.
        </p>

        <p>
          The solution is <m>x &gt; 2</m>. In interval notation, this is <m>(2, \infty)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rmk-ineq-factoring">
      <p>
        The test point method works best when the expression is factored.
        This is why factoring is a critical prerequisite for inequalities. Let's see how this plays out in the next example.
      </p>
    </remark>


    <example>
    <title>Quadratic Example</title> 
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>x^2 - 5x  \le -6</m>. Write your answer in interval notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p> <alert>Step 1:</alert> Rewrite the inequality so one side is zero.
        </p>
        <me>
          x^2 - 5x + 6 \le 0.
        </me>


        <p>
          <alert>Step 2:</alert> Find all values of <m>x</m> where the expression equals zero or is undefined.
        </p>
        <me> 
          \begin{aligned}
            x^2 - 5x + 6 &amp;= 0 \\
            (x - 2)(x - 3) &amp;= 0 \\
            x &amp;= 2 \text{ or } x = 3
          \end{aligned}
        </me>
        <p> There are no values of <m>x</m> where the expression is undefined.</p>

         <p>
          <alert>Step 3:</alert> Use these values to divide the number line into intervals.
        </p>

        <p> The values <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> divide the number line into three intervals: <m>(-\infty, 2)</m>, <m>(2, 3)</m>, and <m>(3, \infty)</m>. 
          Visually this looks like:</p>

          <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided at x equals 2 and x equals 3.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
	\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.5]
		\draw[<->, thick] (0,0)-- 
		(2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- 
		(3,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$3$}-- 
		(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
	\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>
          
        <p>
          <alert>Step 4:</alert> Choose a test point from each interval and determine the sign of the expression.
        </p>

        <ul><li>
          For the interval <m>(-\infty, 2)</m>, we can test <m>x = 0</m>. Plugging this into 
          our expression gives <m>0^2 - 5(0) + 6 = 6</m>, which is positive.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(2, 3)</m>, we can test <m>x = 2.5 = \frac{5}{2}</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>(\tfrac{5}{2})^2 - 5(\tfrac{5}{2}) + 6 = \tfrac{25}{4} - \tfrac{25}{2} + 6 = -\tfrac{1}{4}</m>, 
          which is negative.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(3, \infty)</m>, we can test <m>x = 4</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>4^2 - 5(4) + 6 = 16 - 20 + 6 = 2</m>, which is positive.
        </li></ul>

        <p> Visually, this looks like:</p>

        
          <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided at x equals 2 and x equals 3. The interval to the left of 2 is labeled positive,
    the interval between 2 and 3 is labeled negative, and the interval to the right
    of 3 is labeled positive. Test points at x equals 0, x equals 2.5, and x equals 4 are marked with their respective signs.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
	\begin{tikzpicture}[xscale=1.5]
		\draw[<->, thick] (0,0)-- 
		(2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- 
		(3,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$3$}-- 
		(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
		\draw (1,0) 
		node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(+)$}
		node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$0$};
		\draw (2.5,0) 
		node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(-)$}
		node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$\tfrac52$};
		\draw (4,0) 
		node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(+)$}
		node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$4$};
		
	\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image>


        <p>
          <alert>Step 5:</alert> State your conclusion: Choose the interval(s) where the expression is negative or zero 
          (since the inequality is less than or equal to zero).
        </p>

        <p>
          The expression is negative on the interval <m>(2, 3)</m> and equals zero at <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = 3</m>.
          Since the inequality is less than or equal to zero, we include the endpoints.
          The solution is <m>[2, 3]</m>.
        </p>
        
        
      </solution>
    </example>


  <p>
    Rational inequalities require special care because the expression may be undefined
    for certain values of <m>x</m>.
    As we saw in our technique, these values must always be considered when dividing the number line into intervals.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-ineq-rational">
    <title>Rational Example</title> 
    <statement>
      <p>
        Solve <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{x^2} &gt; 0</m>. Write your answer in interval notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>  <alert>Step 1:</alert> Rewrite the inequality so one side is zero (already done).
      </p>

      <p> <alert>Step 2:</alert> Identify where the expression equals zero or is undefined.
        
        <p>
          The expression is undefined where the denominator is zero, which occurs at <m>x = 0</m>.
          To find where the expression equals zero, we set solve:
        </p>
        <me>
          \begin{aligned}
            1 - \dfrac{4}{x^2} &amp;= 0 \\
            \dfrac{4}{x^2} &amp;= 1 \\
            x^2 &amp;= 4 \\
            x &amp;= \pm 2 
          \end{aligned}
        </me>
        
        </p>


      <p>
        <alert>Step 3:</alert> Use these values to divide the number line into intervals.
      </p>

  =   <p>
        The values <m>-2</m>, <m>0</m>, and <m>2</m> divide the number line into four intervals: <m>(-\infty, -2)</m>, <m>(-2, 0)</m>, <m>(0, 2)</m>, and <m>(2, \infty)</m>. 
          Visually this looks like:</p>


          <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided  at x equals -2, x equals 0, and x equals 2.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
	\draw[<->, thick] (-4,0)-- 
	(-2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$-2$}-- 
	(0,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$0$}-- 
	(2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- 
	(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image> 


        <p>
          <alert>Step 4:</alert> Choose a test point from each interval and determine the sign of the expression.
        </p>
        <ul><li>
          For the interval <m>(-\infty, -2)</m>, we can test <m>x = -3</m>. Plugging this into 
          our expression gives <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{(-3)^2} = 1 - \dfrac{4}{9} = \dfrac{5}{9}</m>, which is positive.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(-2, 0)</m>, we can test <m>x = -1</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{(-1)^2} = 1 - 4 = -3</m>, which is negative.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(0, 2)</m>, we can test <m>x = 1</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{(1)^2} = 1 - 4 = -3</m>, which is negative.
        </li>
        <li>
          For the interval <m>(2, \infty)</m>, we can test <m>x = 3</m>. Plugging this into
          our expression gives <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{(3)^2} = 1 - \dfrac{4}{9} = \dfrac{5}{9}</m>, which is positive.
        </li></ul>

        <p> Visually, this looks like:</p>

        
          <image width="80%">
  <shortdescription>
    Number line divided  at x equals -2, x equals 0, and x equals 2. The interval to the left of -2 is labeled positive, 
    the interval between -2 and 0 is labeled negative, the interval between 0 and 2 is labeled negative, 
    and the interval to the right of 2 is labeled positive. 
    Test points at x equals -3, x equals -1, x equals 1, and x equals 3 are marked with their respective signs.
  </shortdescription>
    <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
	\draw[<->, thick] (-4,0)-- 
	(-2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$-2$}-- 
	(0,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$0$}-- 
	(2,0) node {$|$} node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$2$}-- 
	(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
	\draw (-3,0) 
	node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(+)$}
	node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$-3$};
	\draw (-1,0) 
	node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(-)$}
	node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$-1$};
	\draw (1,0) 
	node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(-)$}
	node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$1$};
	\draw (3,0) 
	node[above, yshift=4pt] {$(+)$}
	node[below, yshift=-4pt] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
 ]]>
    </latex-image>
  </image> 


        <p>
          <alert>Step 5:</alert> State your conclusion: Choose the interval(s) where the expression is positive.
        </p>

        <p>
          The expression is positive on the intervals <m>(-\infty, -2)</m> and <m>(2, \infty)</m>.
          The solution is <m>(-\infty, -2) \cup (2, \infty)</m>.
        </p>  
  
    </solution>
  </example>

</subsection>

  

